# Which provisioning set the packaged builds of this application carry.
#
# Domesday Duplicator - LaserDisc RF sampler
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Inns
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# A packaged build installs one provisioning set beside itself so that a board can be
# brought up with no network — the ordinary case, because a board being brought up is one
# that cannot be updated over USB and may be sitting beside a machine that has just been
# built.
#
# **It is a pin, not a build.** Firmware and the capture application are separate release
# streams (AGENTS.md §9): a gui-v* tag must not rebuild firmware, and a firmware artefact
# must not be assembled by a packaging job. So this file names an existing, published,
# CI-built asset by digest, and the packaging workflows fetch exactly that — through
# tools/fetch-bundled-provisioning.sh, which refuses anything whose SHA-256 differs.
#
# Everything the fetched file claims is still checked at install time by the application
# itself: the signature against the compiled-in release key, then every payload's digest.
# The pin protects the *packaging* step, where there is no key and no user watching; it is
# not a second trust anchor and it replaces none of that.
#
# Updating the pin is one commit, made deliberately after a firmware release:
#
#   1. read the SHA-256 out of that release's SHA256SUMS for the provisioning set;
#   2. set the three values below;
#   3. commit, with the firmware tag in the message.
#
# An empty pin is a legitimate state and means "this build bundles nothing": the packaging
# jobs skip the fetch, the installers are smaller, and the bring-up wizard opens with its
# file picker and no preselection. That is the honest state, and it is the same convention
# the unpinned release key follows — an application that pretended to carry a set it did
# not have would fail at the one moment its user has no network to fix it with.
#
# It is empty today because no firmware release has yet published a provisioning set: the
# `gateware-provisioning-svf` component and the SVF artefact land in the same release that
# first fills this in.

# The firmware release tag the set was published under, e.g. fw-v1.4.0. Recorded so that a
# packaged build can be traced back to one release, and so that the URL below can be
# checked by eye against it.
BUNDLED_PROVISIONING_TAG=""

# The asset to fetch. A full URL rather than an assembled one, because a release asset's
# address is a fact about that release rather than something a packaging job should be
# guessing at from a naming rule.
BUNDLED_PROVISIONING_URL=""

# The SHA-256 of that asset, lowercase hex. This is the value that makes the fetch safe;
# a mismatch fails the packaging job rather than warning.
BUNDLED_PROVISIONING_SHA256=""
